----------------------------------------------
Marker Interface
-----------------------------------------------
Marker interface is an empty interface (no methods)
It is used to mark a class (give special meaning to JVM or compiler)
It provides special behavior or feature to the class
JVM checks the presence of marker interface and changes behavior accordingly
Example: Serializable
If a class implements it → object can be converted into byte stream
Example: Cloneable
If a class implements it → object can be cloned using clone()
Example: RandomAccess
Used to indicate fast random access (like in ArrayList)
Marker interface does not contain any method
Behavior is handled internally by JVM or APIs
use-To avoid writing extra configuration code
Alternative to marker interface is annotations (modern approach)
“Marker interface is an empty interface used to
 provide special instructions to JVM
 ___________________________________________________
🔹 Important Marker Interfaces (Java / Collections Related)

✅ 1. Serializable:
-It is a marker interface used for serialization
-Serialization means → converting object into byte stream
Object → converted into a format that can be:
stored in file/save the objcect
sent over network/transfer the object

-👉 Byte stream means data converted into a sequence of bytes (0s and 1s)
💡 What is a Byte?
1 byte = 8 bits (0s and 1s)
Computers understand only binary (0 and 1)

🔥 Why Convert to Byte Stream?
Because:
File can store only bytes
Network sends only bytes
👉 So object must be converted → byte stream

Student { id = 1, name = "Amit" }
👉 In memory → normal object
👉 After serialization → becomes:
01010100 01101001 00101010 ...
👉 This is called byte stream
🔹 Without Serializable:
Java will throw:
NotSerializableException

🔥 What You Need To Do (Step-by-Step):
✅ Step 1: Implement Serializable
import java.io.Serializable;

class Student implements Serializable {
    int id;
    String name;
}
👉 This tells JVM:
“Object of this class can be converted into byte stream”

✅ Step 2: Write Object to File (Serialization)
import java.io.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        Student s1 = new Student();
        s1.id = 1;
        s1.name = "Amit";

        FileOutputStream file = new FileOutputStream("student.txt");
        ObjectOutputStream out = new ObjectOutputStream(file);

        out.writeObject(s1);  // convert object → byte stream

        out.close();
        file.close();

        System.out.println("Object serialized");
    }
}
👉 What happens here:
s1 object → converted into byte stream
Stored in file student.txt

✅ Step 3: Read Object from File (Deserialization)
import java.io.*;
public class ReadExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        FileInputStream file = new FileInputStream("student.txt");
        ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(file);

        Student s2 = (Student) in.readObject(); // byte stream → object

        in.close();
        file.close();

        System.out.println(s2.name); // Amit
    }
}
👉 Now:
File data → converted back → object

Serializable = permission
writeObject() = conversion to byte stream
readObject() = convert back to object

“To serialize an object, we implement Serializable interface
to allow conversion into byte stream. Then we use ObjectOutputStream
to write the object into a file and ObjectInputStream to read it back.”
+++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

✅ 2. Cloneable
It is a marker interface used for object cloning
Cloning means → creating exact copy of object
One object → create another object with same data

Why we use it:
To create duplicate objects
Avoid creating object manually

Without Cloneable:
Java throws:
CloneNotSupportedException

👉 Just implementing Cloneable is not enough
we need object class clone() method
By default, it is protected
And it does not allow cloning unless class implements Cloneable

🔥 What You Need To Do (Step-by-Step)
✅ Step 1: Implement Cloneable
class Student implements Cloneable {
    int id;
    String name;
}


👉 This tells JVM:
“Cloning is allowed for this class”

✅ Step 2: Override clone() method
class Student implements Cloneable {
    int id;
    String name;

    public Object clone() throws CloneNotSupportedException {
        return super.clone();
    }
}

👉 Why?

To make clone() accessible (public)
To actually perform cloning

✅ Step 3: Use clone()
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        Student s1 = new Student();
        s1.id = 1;
        s1.name = "Amit";

        Student s2 = (Student) s1.clone();

        System.out.println(s1.name); // Amit
        System.out.println(s2.name); // Amit
    }
}
👉 Now:

s2 is a copy of s1
❌ What Happens If You DON’T Implement Cloneable?
👉 Runtime error:
CloneNotSupportedException

💡 Why This Happens?
👉 JVM checks:
“Does this class implement Cloneable?”
👉 If NO:
❌ Throw exception

👉 If YES:
✅ Allow cloning

“To use cloning, we need to implement Cloneable interface
to indicate that cloning is allowed, and override the clone()
method to make it accessible and perform the copy.
Otherwise, Java throws CloneNotSupportedException.”
++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
✅ 3. RandomAccess
It is a marker interface used in collections
Indicates fast index-based access

🔹 What that means (simple):
You can directly access elements using index quickly
👉 Example:
list.get(5)
👉 “This list supports fast direct access”
++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

✅ 4. Externalizable
It is a marker interface for custom serialization
🔹 What that means (simple):
You control:=>what data to save  and how to save
🔹 Why we use it:
More control than Serializable
Improve performance
👉 “I will decide how object should be stored”
+++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
“Marker interfaces are empty interfaces that provide special
capabilities to a class, like Serializable for object storage,
 Cloneable for copying objects,
 and RandomAccess for fast access in collections.”